The plan for this session is written: two task files plus issue_responses.md in session_plan/. The commit didn't happen because .gitignore excludes session_plan/. The files are on disk, and the command's || true suggests the harness expects that and reads them from there. That's my inference; I haven't confirmed it.

Task 1: upgrade yoagent 0.18.1 → 0.24.0 (#987, creator ask, product).
• **Order:** download the 0.24.0 source and read its Breaking sections first, then bump the version.
• **Compile fixes:** `ModelConfig.cost` is now optional. When a preset has no price, it falls back to yoyo's own price table, never to $0. `thinking_level_name` gets arms for the two new thinking levels (XHigh, Max) plus a catch-all.
• **MiniMax:** follow yoagent's new default URL rather than overriding it, with a one-line comment explaining why.
• **The 14 version-claim markers:** each one is re-read against the 0.24 source before its version changes. Two of them change content, not just citations:
  • CLAUDE.md's "high is the top rung" sentence gets marked superseded. yoagent now has XHigh/Max, but yoyo itself still turns max into High.
  • ARCHITECTURE.md's note on sub-agent failures gets corrected for how a cancelled child is reported since 0.23.
• **Size rule:** this task goes over the 3-file limit on purpose, because a dependency bump has to compile and pass in one commit. The task file says so.
• **Out of scope:** making max actually send the Max level (a cost decision), and a live Bedrock test (no credentials).

Task 2: check that yoagent's own retry doesn't print `-p` output twice (product).
• **Why a green suite can't answer this:** since 0.23, yoagent retries inside itself after part of an answer has already been printed. The Day-216 fix that stopped duplicate output lives in yoyo's own retry loop, so it can't see those retries.
• **Two stub tests**, each checking the full stdout and the exit code:
  • The server fails before any text, then succeeds. Retry should still work here, so this case guards against breaking it.
  • The server sends part of an answer, dies, then succeeds.
• **What happens next depends on the result:** if both are clean, pin them with a headline that says only that this specific hypothesis was ruled out. If either reproduces, the fix stays within three files; if it needs more, ship the failing tests as ignored tests tied to a filed issue.
• **If Task 1 doesn't land,** Task 2 still runs and records the 0.18 behaviour as the "before" reading.

